---
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// PageHeader — the top of every major authenticated page (§12, §13, §15, §104)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// PURPOSE
//
// §12 says every major authenticated page opens the same way: breadcrumb,
// title, description, status and metadata, one primary action, the secondary
// actions, tabs. Written by hand that ordering survives about four pages. The
// twenty-five admin screens already share a shell; this is the part of the page
// the shell cannot supply, because only the page knows its own title and its
// own actions.
//
// WHY THE PROPS ARE SHAPED THE WAY THEY ARE
//
// §13 — one dominant action per screen — is the rule this component exists to
// make unbreakable. It is not enforced by a comment or a code review, because
// neither survives a deadline. It is enforced by the TYPE: `primary` is a
// single object, not an array, so a page that wants six primary actions cannot
// express it. Everything else goes in `secondary`, and past `collapseAfter`
// (two, by default) the remainder collapses into a "More" disclosure. A
// component that permits the mistake will eventually be given it.
//
// PROPS
//
//   breadcrumbs   Crumb[] from src/lib/seo.ts — the SAME type the sitemap and
//                 the BreadcrumbList structured data already use, so a page
//                 declares its trail once. The last entry is the current page
//                 (the convention every page in src/pages already follows);
//                 every entry before it is a link (§15). Fewer than two entries
//                 renders NOTHING, matching breadcrumbGraph(): a trail of one
//                 is not a hierarchy, it is decoration.
//   title         The page. Rendered as the h1 unless `headingLevel` says
//                 otherwise.
//   headingLevel  'h1' (default) or 'h2'. A shell that already prints an h1
//                 must be given 'h2', or the page has two.
//   description   One sentence on what this page is for. Optional, and absent
//                 is better than padded.
//   primary       ONE action. See above.
//   secondary     The rest. Order matters: the first `collapseAfter` stay in
//                 the bar, the remainder go behind "More".
//   collapseAfter Default 2.
//   tabs          Real links with real hrefs (§79, §80). Never JS state.
//   sticky        Default true. Turn it off on a short page where a sticky bar
//                 is just a bar.
//   id            Anchor for skip links and deep links.
//
//   slot "meta"   Counts, chips and statuses. A status goes in here as
//                 <Status …/> and in no other way — that component is the only
//                 place a database enum becomes a word a person reads.
//
// STATES
//
//   no breadcrumbs / one crumb    the trail is not rendered at all.
//   no description                the row is absent, not an empty paragraph.
//   no primary                    the action bar renders only what exists; a
//                                 page with nothing to do has no bar.
//   an unavailable action         rendered as an aria-disabled button carrying
//                                 the REASON, not hidden and not a dead link.
//                                 An action that vanishes teaches nobody why.
//   no tabs                       no tab strip, and no border where one would
//                                 have been.
//   stuck to the top              a shadow appears, and nothing else changes.
//
// ACCESSIBILITY (§52, WCAG 2.2 AA)
//
//   · The trail is a <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"> with an ordered list; the
//     current page is aria-current="page" and is NOT a link, because a link to
//     where you already are is a keyboard stop that does nothing.
//   · Tabs are a <nav> of links, not role="tablist". The ARIA tab pattern
//     promises panels swapped by script under one URL; these are separate URLs
//     that survive a refresh and can be pasted to a colleague. Announcing them
//     as tabs would describe behaviour this component deliberately does not
//     have. The current one carries aria-current="page".
//   · "More" is a native <details>/<summary> disclosure. It therefore works
//     with JavaScript off, announces its own expanded state, and is operable
//     with Enter and Space without a line of script. Script only ADDS Escape,
//     click-outside, focus restored to the summary, and arrow-key movement.
//   · There is NO focus trap in the More menu, on purpose. §52 asks for a trap
//     in overlays; a disclosure is not an overlay — it does not cover the page
//     and Tab must be able to leave it. Trapping here would strand the keyboard
//     in a three-item list. Focus leaving the menu closes it instead.
//   · An unavailable action uses aria-disabled, never the `disabled` attribute:
//     a disabled button cannot be focused, so the reason it is disabled can
//     never be read by the person most likely to need it.
//   · Focus is never obscured (2.4.11): the sticky offset defaults to the same
//     96px band global.css already reserves with scroll-padding-top.
//
// RESPONSIVE
//
//   Below 900px the title and the primary action stay — they are the page and
//   the thing to do on it. The inline secondary actions collapse into "More",
//   which then holds all of them. Each action exists twice in the markup, but
//   exactly one copy is display:none at any width, so nothing is announced
//   twice and nothing is a hidden tab stop. The tab strip scrolls sideways
//   inside itself; the page body never scrolls sideways.
//
// DO
//   · Give it one primary action and let the rest be secondary.
//   · Put counts in the meta slot only when you have counted something.
//   · Point tabs at querystrings — ?status=open — so the view is shareable.
// DON'T
//   · Don't render a status any way but <Status>.
//   · Don't invent a count, a total or a deadline to fill the meta slot. If the
//     number would have to be made up, the slot stays empty.
//   · Don't pass a title here when the surrounding shell already prints one.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

import type { Crumb } from '@/lib/seo';

/**
 * How many times this component has rendered in this process. Feeds the
 * per-instance id below; see the comment there for why a title hash was not
 * enough.
 *
 * It has to be EXPORTED. Astro's compiler hoists a top-level export out of the
 * component function and into module scope, and leaves an ordinary `let` inside
 * it — an ordinary `let` would therefore be reset to zero on every single
 * render and count nothing. Nothing outside this file should read it.
 */
export let phInstances = 0;

export interface PageAction {
  label: string;
  href: string;
  /** Why this cannot be done right now. Present = rendered as refused, with
   *  the reason attached, rather than quietly removed from the bar. */
  unavailable?: string;
  /** Irreversible, or hard to undo. Marked in words as well as colour. */
  danger?: boolean;
  /** Serves a file rather than a page. */
  download?: boolean | string;
}

export interface PageTab {
  label: string;
  href: string;
  /** A real count from a real query. Omit it rather than guess — a zero nobody
   *  counted reads exactly like a zero somebody did. */
  count?: number;
  /** Force the current tab. Left alone, the current URL decides. */
  current?: boolean;
}

export interface Props {
  breadcrumbs?: Crumb[];
  title: string;
  headingLevel?: 'h1' | 'h2';
  description?: string;
  primary?: PageAction;
  secondary?: PageAction[];
  collapseAfter?: number;
  tabs?: PageTab[];
  sticky?: boolean;
  id?: string;
}

const {
  breadcrumbs = [],
  title,
  headingLevel = 'h1',
  description,
  primary,
  secondary = [],
  collapseAfter = 2,
  tabs = [],
  sticky = true,
  id,
} = Astro.props;

const Heading = headingLevel;

// A crumb without both halves is not a crumb. Filtering before the length test
// means a half-filled trail degrades to nothing rather than to a broken link.
const trail: Crumb[] = (breadcrumbs ?? []).filter((c) => c && c.name && c.url);
const showTrail = trail.length >= 2;

const acts: PageAction[] = (secondary ?? []).filter((a) => a && a.label && a.href);
// Never fewer than zero kept, and a page that asks for everything inline still
// gets a working bar — the mobile rules below fold it away regardless.
const keep = Math.max(0, collapseAfter);
const inline = acts.slice(0, keep);
const overflow = acts.slice(keep);
// The disclosure holds EVERY secondary action, because below 900px it is the
// only place any of them live. Above 900px the duplicates of the inline ones
// are display:none, which removes them from the accessibility tree too.
const menu = acts;
const hasMenu = menu.length > 0;

// ── which tab is current ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Derived from the URL rather than declared, so a page cannot ship with two
// tabs marked current or with none. `current` on the tab still wins, for the
// pages where the mapping is not mechanical.
//
// A tab href may differ from the current URL only by querystring — ?status=open
// — so a path comparison alone would mark every tab current. A tab matches when
// its path matches AND every parameter it names is satisfied; the tab that
// names the most parameters is the most specific, and wins.
const here = Astro.url;
const strip = (p: string) => p.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '/';

function specificity(t: PageTab): number {
  let u: URL;
  try {
    u = new URL(t.href, here);
  } catch {
    return -1;
  }
  if (strip(u.pathname) !== strip(here.pathname)) return -1;
  const params = [...u.searchParams];
  for (const [k, v] of params) if (here.searchParams.get(k) !== v) return -1;
  return params.length;
}

const tabList: PageTab[] = (tabs ?? []).filter((t) => t && t.label && t.href);
let currentTab = tabList.findIndex((t) => t.current === true);
if (currentTab < 0) {
  let best = -1;
  tabList.forEach((t, i) => {
    const s = specificity(t);
    if (s > best) {
      best = s;
      currentTab = i;
    }
  });
  if (best < 0) currentTab = -1;
}

// A collision-free id per INSTANCE for the aria-describedby wiring on refused
// actions.
//
// It was a hash of the title alone. A hash of the title is not an instance id:
// two headers on one page with the same title — a record page and a section
// below it, the same screen rendered twice in a comparison — produce the same
// hash, and then two elements share one id. aria-describedby resolves to the
// first match in the document, so the second header's refusal would announce
// the first header's reason. Wrong text on a refusal is worse than no text.
//
// The counter is module-scope, so it increments across every render this server
// process performs. That makes the id unstable between page loads, which costs
// nothing: nothing links to it and no client framework rehydrates against it.
// Uniqueness within one document is the only property required.
const uid = 'ph-' + (phInstances++).toString(36) + '-' + Math.abs(
  String(title).split('').reduce((a, c) => ((a * 31 + c.charCodeAt(0)) | 0), 7)
).toString(36);
---

<header
  class:list={['ph', sticky && 'ph-sticky']}
  id={id}
  data-page-header
>
  <div class="ph-inner">

    {showTrail && (
      <nav class="ph-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol>
          {trail.map((c, i) =>
            i === trail.length - 1 ? (
              <li><span aria-current="page">{c.name}</span></li>
            ) : (
              <li><a href={c.url}>{c.name}</a></li>
            )
          )}
        </ol>
      </nav>
    )}

    <div class="ph-bar">
      <div class="ph-id">
        <Heading class="ph-title">{title}</Heading>
        {Astro.slots.has('meta') && (
          <div class="ph-meta"><slot name="meta" /></div>
        )}
      </div>

      {(primary || acts.length > 0) && (
        <div class="ph-actions">
          {inline.map((a, i) =>
            a.unavailable ? (
              // The button and the sentence explaining it share ONE wrapper, so
              // the width rule that folds the bar away takes the reason with it.
              // Left as a bare sibling, the .sr-only reason would still be read
              // out on a phone where its button no longer exists.
              <span class="ph-inline-act ph-inline-off">
                <button
                  type="button"
                  class="ph-btn ph-btn-ghost ph-btn-off"
                  aria-disabled="true"
                  aria-describedby={`${uid}-off-${i}`}
                >{a.label}</button>
                <span id={`${uid}-off-${i}`} class="sr-only">{a.unavailable}</span>
              </span>
            ) : (
              <a
                class:list={['ph-btn', 'ph-btn-ghost', 'ph-inline-act', a.danger && 'ph-btn-danger']}
                href={a.href}
                download={a.download === true ? '' : (a.download || undefined)}
              >{a.label}</a>
            )
          )}

          {hasMenu && (
            <details class:list={['ph-more', overflow.length === 0 && 'ph-more-narrow']} data-ph-more>
              <summary class="ph-btn ph-btn-ghost ph-more-btn">
                <span>More</span>
                <span class="sr-only"> actions</span>
                <svg class="ph-chev" width="10" height="6" viewBox="0 0 10 6" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                </svg>
              </summary>
              <ul class="ph-more-list">
                {menu.map((a, i) => (
                  <li class:list={[i < keep && 'ph-dupe', a.unavailable && 'ph-more-off']}>
                    {a.unavailable ? (
                      // The reason sits OUTSIDE the button on purpose. Inside,
                      // it would become part of the button's accessible name
                      // and then be announced a second time by describedby —
                      // the label read twice with the explanation buried in it.
                      <>
                        <button
                          type="button"
                          class="ph-more-item ph-btn-off"
                          aria-disabled="true"
                          aria-describedby={`${uid}-moff-${i}`}
                        >{a.label}</button>
                        <span class="ph-why" id={`${uid}-moff-${i}`}>{a.unavailable}</span>
                      </>
                    ) : (
                      <a
                        class:list={['ph-more-item', a.danger && 'ph-item-danger']}
                        href={a.href}
                        download={a.download === true ? '' : (a.download || undefined)}
                      >{a.label}</a>
                    )}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </details>
          )}

          {primary && (
            primary.unavailable ? (
              <div class="ph-primary-off">
                <button
                  type="button"
                  class="ph-btn ph-btn-primary ph-btn-off"
                  aria-disabled="true"
                  aria-describedby={`${uid}-primary-off`}
                >{primary.label}</button>
                <p class="ph-why" id={`${uid}-primary-off`}>{primary.unavailable}</p>
              </div>
            ) : (
              <a
                class:list={['ph-btn', 'ph-btn-primary', primary.danger && 'ph-btn-danger-solid']}
                href={primary.href}
                download={primary.download === true ? '' : (primary.download || undefined)}
              >{primary.label}</a>
            )
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>

    {description && <p class="ph-desc">{description}</p>}

    {tabList.length > 0 && (
      <nav class="ph-tabs" aria-label="Views of this page">
        <ul>
          {tabList.map((t, i) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={t.href}
                class={i === currentTab ? 'is-current' : undefined}
                aria-current={i === currentTab ? 'page' : undefined}
              >
                <span>{t.label}</span>
                {/* Only a number that was actually counted. `count` left
                    undefined prints nothing — it never falls back to 0. */}
                {typeof t.count === 'number' && <span class="ph-count">{t.count}</span>}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    )}
  </div>
</header>

<style>
  .ph {
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  /* The offset is the SAME 96px band global.css already reserves with
     scroll-padding-top for WCAG 2.4.11, so a focused element scrolled into view
     lands below this header rather than behind it. A shell that does not sit
     under the fixed site nav overrides --ph-sticky-top rather than editing
     this rule. */
  .ph-sticky {
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--ph-sticky-top, var(--space-24));
    z-index: var(--z-sticky);
  }

  /* Stuck state changes the SHADOW and nothing else. Shrinking a sticky header
     on scroll changes its height in normal flow, which moves everything below
     it, which can move the header back out of its stuck range — a header that
     flickers between two sizes for the whole length of a long list. A shadow
     costs no layout. */
  .ph-sticky.is-stuck { box-shadow: var(--shadow-md); }

  .ph-inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-5) 0 0;
  }

  /* ── breadcrumb ── */
  .ph-crumbs ol {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    list-style: none;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .ph-crumbs li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .ph-crumbs li + li::before {
    content: '/';
    /* Matches the breadcrumb separator in Base.astro, which uses --gold-dim.
       --muted-2 is 1.87:1 and is decorative-only by declaration in global.css. */
    color: var(--gold-dim);
  }
  .ph-crumbs a {
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    transition: color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out),
                border-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  .ph-crumbs a:hover { color: var(--gold-2); border-bottom-color: var(--gold-dim); }
  .ph-crumbs [aria-current='page'] { color: var(--off-white); }

  /* ── title row ── */
  .ph-bar {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .ph-id {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .ph-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    color: var(--white);
    /* A long register name must wrap rather than push the primary action off
       the right edge of a narrow screen. */
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .ph-meta { display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); }

  .ph-desc {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: var(--max-w-prose);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  /* ── actions ── */
  .ph-actions {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    /* The dominant action sits last in the DOM so it is the last thing a
       keyboard reaches before the content, and last in the visual row on the
       right — but row-reverse would fight the source order, so the order is
       simply authored as it reads. */
  }

  /* Local sizing rather than the site .btn, which is a 30px-padded call to
     action built for a marketing page. An operations header needs a control. */
  .ph-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out),
                border-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out),
                color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  /* --bg-2 rather than a literal white: the palette is fixed, and a hex here is
     a colour nobody can find again from the token scale. */
  .ph-btn-primary { background: var(--red); color: var(--bg-2); }
  .ph-btn-primary:hover { background: var(--red-2); color: var(--bg-2); }
  /* --control-border, not --border-2: a bordered control's edge IS the thing
     that identifies it, so it has to clear 3:1 (WCAG 1.4.11). */
  .ph-btn-ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--off-white); border-color: var(--control-border); }
  .ph-btn-ghost:hover { border-color: var(--gold); color: var(--gold-3); }
  /* The rule two lines above applies to THIS button too, and it did not.
     --red-3 is declared in global.css as a hairline for rules and pill edges
     and measures 1.49:1 on white; on a transparent ghost button the border is
     the only boundary there is, so a destructive action was the one control in
     this header whose edge a low-vision reader could not find. --red is 9.3:1
     on the header ground and reads as the danger outline it already meant. */
  .ph-btn-danger { color: var(--red); border-color: var(--red); }
  .ph-btn-danger:hover { color: var(--bg-2); background: var(--red); border-color: var(--red); }
  .ph-btn-danger-solid { background: var(--red); color: var(--bg-2); }

  /* Refused, not removed. Hue alone never carries this (1.4.1): the fill and
     the ink weight both change, and the reason is real text on aria-describedby
     — visible text in the menu and on the primary, screen-reader-only on the
     inline copy, where the bar has no room for a sentence.

     NO opacity. This carries aria-disabled rather than `disabled` precisely so
     it stays focusable and its reason stays readable; 0.6 opacity took --muted
     on --card-2 from 4.63:1 down to roughly 2.5:1, which made the label of the
     one control that needs explaining the hardest thing in the header to read.
     The refusal is shown by the filled ground and the muted ink instead, both
     of which survive at full strength. */
  .ph-btn-off {
    cursor: not-allowed;
    background: var(--card-2);
    color: var(--muted);
    border-color: var(--control-border);
  }
  .ph-btn-off:hover { background: var(--card-2); color: var(--muted); border-color: var(--control-border); }

  .ph-primary-off { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); align-items: flex-end; }
  .ph-why {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 34ch;
    text-align: right;
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-normal);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
  }

  /* ── the More disclosure ── */
  .ph-more { position: relative; }
  .ph-more-btn { list-style: none; }
  /* Safari draws its own triangle on a summary and ignores list-style. */
  .ph-more-btn::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .ph-more[open] .ph-chev { transform: rotate(180deg); }
  .ph-chev { transition: transform var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out); }

  .ph-more-list {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: calc(100% + var(--space-2));
    z-index: var(--z-dropdown);
    min-width: 220px;
    max-width: min(320px, calc(100vw - var(--space-8)));
    list-style: none;
    padding: var(--space-2);
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-lg);
  }
  .ph-more-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    width: 100%;
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-xs);
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--off-white);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-align: left;
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .ph-more-item:hover { background: var(--card-2); color: var(--white); }
  .ph-item-danger { color: var(--red); }
  .ph-item-danger:hover { color: var(--red-2); background: var(--card-2); }
  .ph-more-item.ph-btn-off { background: transparent; color: var(--muted); }
  .ph-more-off { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); }
  .ph-more-off .ph-why {
    text-align: left;
    max-width: none;
    padding: 0 var(--space-3) var(--space-2);
  }
  .ph-inline-off { display: inline-flex; }

  /* ── tabs ──
     Links, not a tablist. See the note at the top of this file. */
  .ph-tabs {
    overflow-x: auto;
    /* The strip carries its own sideways scroll so the page body never does —
       the failure that makes an operations screen unusable on a phone.
       overflow-x: auto forces overflow-y to compute to auto as well, so the
       strip clips VERTICALLY too — and the global focus ring is a 2px outline
       at a 2px offset, which meant the top and bottom of the ring on a focused
       tab were being cut off by the scroller (2.4.7, 2.4.11). The padding gives
       the ring room inside the scrolling box; the negative bottom margin pulls
       the current tab's underline back onto the header's own bottom edge, so
       nothing moves. */
    padding: var(--space-1) 0;
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--space-1) * -1);
    scrollbar-width: thin;
  }
  .ph-tabs ul {
    display: flex;
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    list-style: none;
    min-width: max-content;
  }
  .ph-tabs a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-3) 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
    /* The underline must not be the only signal, so the current tab also
       changes weight and colour. */
    transition: color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out),
                border-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }
  .ph-tabs a:hover { color: var(--off-white); border-bottom-color: var(--border-2); }
  .ph-tabs a.is-current {
    color: var(--red);
    font-weight: 600;
    border-bottom-color: var(--red);
  }
  .ph-count {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: var(--space-5);
    padding: 0 var(--space-2);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--card-2);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ph-tabs a.is-current .ph-count { background: var(--red-3); color: var(--red); }

  /* ── responsive ──
     Above 900px: the first `collapseAfter` secondaries are buttons in the bar
     and their copies inside More are removed. Below 900px the reverse. Exactly
     one copy of each action is in the accessibility tree at any width, because
     display:none removes an element from it entirely. */
  .ph-dupe { display: none; }
  .ph-more-narrow { display: none; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .ph-inner { padding-top: var(--space-4); gap: var(--space-2); }
    .ph-title { font-size: var(--text-xl); }
    .ph-inline-act { display: none; }
    .ph-dupe { display: block; }
    .ph-more-narrow { display: block; }
    /* Title and primary action keep the row; everything else has folded away. */
    .ph-bar { flex-wrap: nowrap; align-items: center; }
    .ph-actions { flex-wrap: nowrap; }
    .ph-primary-off { align-items: flex-start; }
    .ph-why { text-align: left; }
  }

  /* Touch targets (2.5.8). global.css already lifts .btn to 44px on small
     screens; these are local controls and need the same floor. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .ph-btn { min-height: 44px; }
    .ph-more-item { min-height: 44px; justify-content: center; }
    .ph-tabs a { min-height: 44px; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Progressive enhancement only.
  //
  // Everything below is an accelerator on top of markup that already works:
  // <details>/<summary> opens, closes, announces its state and responds to
  // Enter and Space with no script at all, and the sticky header is a CSS
  // property. With JavaScript off the page loses a drop shadow and the habit of
  // closing a menu on Escape. It loses no action and no information.

  // ── the More disclosure ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLDetailsElement>('details[data-ph-more]').forEach((d) => {
    const summary = d.querySelector('summary');
    // What the arrow keys may land on: every menu item that is actually on the
    // screen at this width.
    //
    // This used to read `li:not(.ph-dupe)`, which is the exact inverse of what
    // is wanted below 900px. `.ph-dupe` marks the menu copies of the actions
    // that sit inline in the bar on a wide screen — and below 900px the inline
    // bar is gone and those copies are the ONLY place those actions exist. The
    // selector was therefore skipping precisely the items a phone user can see,
    // so Home went to the wrong item, the wrap-around was wrong, and when every
    // secondary fitted inline (nothing in the overflow at all) the list came
    // back empty and the arrow keys did nothing whatsoever.
    //
    // offsetParent alone is the correct test and was already here: it is null
    // for anything under display:none, which is whichever copy the media query
    // has removed at the current width, at either width.
    const items = () =>
      Array.from(
        d.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.ph-more-list li > a, .ph-more-list li > button')
      ).filter((el) => el.offsetParent !== null);

    const close = (restoreFocus: boolean) => {
      if (!d.open) return;
      d.open = false;
      // Focus goes back to the control that opened the menu (§52). Without
      // this, dismissing a menu drops the keyboard at the top of the document
      // and the reader has to tab back through the whole header.
      if (restoreFocus && summary instanceof HTMLElement) summary.focus();
    };

    d.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
      const key = (e as KeyboardEvent).key;
      if (key === 'Escape') {
        // Only swallow Escape when there is a menu open for it to close.
        // stopPropagation used to run first, unconditionally: with the menu
        // shut and focus resting on the summary, this header ate the Escape
        // that a surrounding dialog was waiting for, and the dialog stayed
        // open with no indication why.
        if (!d.open) return;
        e.stopPropagation();
        close(true);
        return;
      }
      if (!d.open) return;
      const list = items();
      if (list.length === 0) return;
      const at = list.indexOf(document.activeElement as HTMLElement);
      if (key === 'ArrowDown' || key === 'ArrowUp') {
        e.preventDefault();
        const step = key === 'ArrowDown' ? 1 : -1;
        // From the summary, ArrowDown enters at the top and ArrowUp at the end.
        const next = at < 0 ? (step === 1 ? 0 : list.length - 1) : (at + step + list.length) % list.length;
        list[next].focus();
      } else if (key === 'Home') {
        e.preventDefault();
        list[0].focus();
      } else if (key === 'End') {
        e.preventDefault();
        list[list.length - 1].focus();
      }
    });

    // NOT a focus trap. This is a disclosure, not a modal: Tab must be able to
    // leave, and leaving is what closes it.
    d.addEventListener('focusout', () => {
      window.setTimeout(() => {
        if (!d.contains(document.activeElement)) close(false);
      }, 0);
    });

    document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      if (d.open && !d.contains(e.target as Node)) close(false);
    });
  });

  // ── stuck shadow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // A scroll listener would run on every frame of every scroll on a long
  // register. The observer fires twice: once on the way down, once on the way
  // back up.
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-page-header].ph-sticky').forEach((el) => {
    if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;
    const top = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(el).top) || 0;
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => el.classList.toggle('is-stuck', entry.intersectionRatio < 1),
      { threshold: [1], rootMargin: `-${top + 1}px 0px 0px 0px` }
    );
    io.observe(el);
  });
</script>
